# A hard stop must carry a route (panel 059, ratified 2026-08-15; §4.17).
#
# SYMPTOM this pins: `print` on a `cstr` stopped with `bad_operand` and said only
# that a `cstr` is not a `str` — which the reader can already see. Panel 058's blind
# reader met that stop, could find no conversion in the specification, and reached
# for a C helper with a `static char[4096]`. That escape is one line long, invisible
# to every rule this compiler has, and strictly worse than what the stop refused.
#
# So the note names the operation and both ways it can fail, which is what §4.17 asks:
# an error carries what is needed to repair the program without opening another file.
#
# The note named `to_str` and a null test until panel 089 (2026-08-24), and that was
# the shape the sitting existed to fix: it sent the reader through a guard that does
# not save them, because `to_str` also aborts on bytes that are not UTF-8.
#
# It is a **note and not a `Fix`**: the repair is `v.validated()`, and building that
# replacement needs the operand's own source text, which the diagnostics module does
# not have. A `Fix` with a span and no text would be worse than none (CLAUDE.md §8).

extern "stdlib.h"
    function getenv(name: cstr) -> cstr

function main()
    v = getenv(name: "HOME".cstr())
    print(v)   #~ bad_operand
